1. WHAT IS A PIPNODE PLUGIN?
----------------------------

Pipnode is a desktop visual flow editor: the user drags "nodes"
onto a worksheet and wires their outputs to the inputs of other
nodes to build a data-processing graph.  Each node type is a C
class derived from `PnNode` (a `GObject` subclass) and registered
with the process-wide `PnNodeFactory` so that the palette, the
JSON load path, and the worksheet renderer can find it by name.

A plugin is a shared object (`.so` on Linux, `.dylib` on macOS,
`.dll` on Windows) that contains one or more `PnNode` subclasses
and registers them with the factory at start-up.  From the user's
point of view a plugin-shipped node is indistinguishable from a
built-in: it appears in the palette, can be saved into a worksheet
JSON file, and round-trips through load and save just like every
other node.

Plugins exist so that:

  - third parties can ship node types without forking pipnode;
  - the project itself can deliver node types in separately
    versioned shared objects (the bundled `pipnode-network` plugin
    that ships Ping and DNS Check is exactly this);
  - downstream packagers can choose which optional node types to
    install on a given machine.


2. HOW DYNAMIC LOADING WORKS (BACKGROUND)
-----------------------------------------

If you have never written a dynamically loaded library, here is
the short version.

A regular executable is linked at build time against a fixed list
of shared libraries.  The list is recorded in the ELF header and
the dynamic linker (`/lib64/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2`) resolves them
when the program starts.  After that, no more code can join the
process unless the program explicitly asks for it.

The POSIX `dlopen(3)` family of calls is how a program asks for
more code at run time:

  - `dlopen("path/to/lib.so", RTLD_NOW)`         opens the file
  - `dlsym(handle, "symbol_name")`               looks up a symbol
  - `dlclose(handle)`                            unmaps the file

Pipnode does not call `dlopen` directly.  Instead it uses GLib's
portable wrapper, `GModule`, which speaks `dlopen` on POSIX,
`LoadLibrary` on Windows, and `NSAddImage` on older macOS -- same
contract, three backends.  The relevant calls are:

  - `g_module_open(path, flags)`        wraps `dlopen`
  - `g_module_symbol(mod, name, &fn)`   wraps `dlsym`
  - `g_module_make_resident(mod)`       pins the module for the
                                        rest of the process's life
                                        so it cannot be unloaded
  - `g_module_close(mod)`               wraps `dlclose`

The "make resident" step matters because pipnode registers
`GType` instances inside the plugin's `pn_plugin_init`, and GLib
does not support unregistering a `GType`.  If the plugin's `.so`
were ever unmapped while a node it registered was still in use,
the next call into the type's vtable would jump into freed
memory and crash the process.  Plugins are therefore a one-way
door: once loaded they stay loaded until the process exits.


3. THE PLUGIN ABI IN ONE PAGE
-----------------------------

The contract between pipnode and a plugin is defined in the
public header `<pipnode/pn-plugin.h>`, which itself pulls in
`<pipnode/pn-node-factory.h>` so the entry point can register
types with the factory.  There are exactly three pieces.

  (a) A constant, the ABI version:

        #define PN_PLUGIN_ABI_VERSION 1

      Bumped whenever an incompatible change to `PnNode`,
      `PnNodeClass`, the factory registration contract, or the
      entry-point signature ships in a release.  Plugins stamp
      this value into the `PnPluginInfo` they return; the host
      refuses to load a plugin whose declared version differs.

  (b) An identity struct that the plugin returns:

        typedef struct
        {
            int          abi_version;
            const gchar *name;
            const gchar *version;
            const gchar *description;
        } PnPluginInfo;

      `name` is required and shows up in the inspector as the
      provenance string for every type the plugin registers.
      `version` is required and free-form ("1.0.0", "2025-04-12").
      `description` is optional.  All three string fields must
      have static lifetime: typically they are string literals
      inside a static const struct.

  (c) An entry-point function that the plugin must export under
      the symbol name "pn_plugin_init":

        typedef const PnPluginInfo *
        (*PnPluginInitFunc) (PnNodeFactory *factory);

      The host calls this once with the process-wide
      `PnNodeFactory`.  The plugin calls
      `pn_node_factory_register(factory, MY_TYPE_FOO)` for each
      `PnNode` subclass it provides and returns a borrowed
      pointer to a static `PnPluginInfo`.  Returning NULL is
      treated as a failed load.

That is the entire contract.  No vtable, no init/fini pair, no
context object -- one symbol, one struct, one constant.


4. WRITING A MINIMAL PLUGIN
---------------------------

A complete, working plugin fits in roughly fifty lines of C.
The file `tests/plugins/echo/pn-echo.c` in the source tree is
exactly such a plugin and is the easiest place to start reading.
A condensed version, with explanatory comments, follows.

    /*  my-echo.c  ---  pass-through demo node  */

    #include <gmodule.h>

    #include <pipnode/pn-node.h>
    #include <pipnode/pn-message.h>
    #include <pipnode/pn-node-factory.h>
    #include <pipnode/pn-plugin.h>

    /*  --- 1.  Declare the GType. --- */

    #define MY_TYPE_ECHO (my_echo_get_type ())

    G_DECLARE_FINAL_TYPE (MyEcho, my_echo, MY, ECHO, PnNode)

    struct _MyEcho
    {
        PnNode parent_instance;
    };

    G_DEFINE_TYPE (MyEcho, my_echo, PN_TYPE_NODE)

    /*  --- 2.  Override the receive vfunc. --- */

    static void
    my_echo_receive (PnNode *node, PnMessage *message)
    {
        /* Forward every incoming message unchanged. */
        pn_node_emit_message (node, message);
    }

    /*  --- 3.  Pin class-level metadata. --- */

    static void
    my_echo_class_init (MyEchoClass *klass)
    {
        PnNodeClass *nc = PN_NODE_CLASS (klass);

        nc->receive    = my_echo_receive;
        nc->class_name = "Echo";              /* palette label   */
        nc->icon       = "\xef\x81\xa4";      /* fa-share glyph  */
        nc->color      = (GdkRGBA){0.55, 0.65, 0.95, 1.0};
        nc->category   = "Filters";           /* palette group   */
        nc->has_input  = TRUE;
        nc->has_output = TRUE;
    }

    static void
    my_echo_init (MyEcho *self)
    {
        (void) self;   /* nothing to initialise per instance */
    }

    /*  --- 4.  Export the plugin entry point. --- */

    G_MODULE_EXPORT const PnPluginInfo *
    pn_plugin_init (PnNodeFactory *factory)
    {
        static const PnPluginInfo info = {
            .abi_version = PN_PLUGIN_ABI_VERSION,
            .name        = "EchoPlugin",
            .version     = "1.0.0",
            .description = "Pass-through demo node.",
        };

        pn_node_factory_register (factory, MY_TYPE_ECHO);
        return &info;
    }

A few notes on the four blocks above.

(1) The `G_DECLARE_FINAL_TYPE` macro from GLib produces all the
    boilerplate for a non-derivable GObject subclass: the
    `MyEcho` instance struct, the `MyEchoClass` class struct, the
    `my_echo_get_type ()` accessor, the `MY_ECHO ()` /
    `MY_IS_ECHO ()` casts, and so on.  Use `G_DECLARE_DERIVABLE_TYPE`
    instead if your node type is meant to be subclassed.

(2) `receive` is the only `PnNodeClass` virtual method most node
    types need to override.  It runs whenever a wire delivers a
    message to this node's input port.  To emit a message on the
    output port (for downstream nodes to receive), call
    `pn_node_emit_message (node, message)`.

(3) The `class_name`, `icon`, `color`, `category`, `has_input`,
    and `has_output` fields are read by the palette and the
    worksheet renderer to draw your node.  They live on the
    class struct rather than the instance because every instance
    of `MyEcho` looks the same; pinning them once during
    `_class_init` saves an allocation per node.

(4) `G_MODULE_EXPORT` annotates the function so the linker keeps
    the symbol visible to `dlsym`.  On most ELF toolchains this
    is a no-op (symbols default to public), but on Windows it
    expands to `__declspec(dllexport)` and is therefore required
    for portability.  The function name must be exactly
    `pn_plugin_init` -- that is the symbol the host looks up, and
    the macro `PN_PLUGIN_INIT_SYMBOL` in `<pn-plugin.h>` is the
    single source of truth.

A plugin can register more than one type from a single
`pn_plugin_init`; the bundled network plugin registers `PnPing`
and `PnDns` from one entry point, for example.


4a. NODES WITH MORE THAN ONE INPUT
----------------------------------

Most nodes have a single input port, which the `has_input`
boolean turns on.  A node can instead expose several input ports
by calling `pn_node_set_n_inputs (node, N)` in its `_init` (this
also turns `has_input` on).  The worksheet then draws N input
tabs stacked down the node's left edge, and each tab can be wired
independently.

Because there is still one `receive` vfunc, a multi-input node
tells its ports apart by asking which one the current message
arrived on:

    static void
    my_blend_receive (PnNode *node, PnMessage *message)
    {
        gint input = pn_node_current_input ();   /* 0 .. N-1 */
        /* ... store/act on the message for that port ... */
    }

`pn_node_current_input ()` is valid only for the duration of the
`receive` call.  A node that must combine its inputs (rather than
react to each independently) typically latches the most recent
message per port and only acts once every port has one; the
bundled image plugin's `pn_image_node_process2 ()` helper does
exactly this for its two-input `Blend` node.

Connections to a specific input survive save/load: a wire to a
port other than 0 stores a `target_input` integer in the
`connections` array (omitted, and assumed 0, for the ordinary
single-input case).  Wires created in C address a port with
`pn_wire_new_full (source, target, target_input)`;
`pn_wire_new (source, target)` is the `target_input == 0` shortcut.


5. BUILDING THE PLUGIN: STANDALONE MAKEFILE
-------------------------------------------

The simplest possible out-of-tree build needs `pkg-config` to
locate pipnode's headers and link line, plus the standard
`-fPIC -shared` toolchain flags for a position-independent
shared object.  A minimal Makefile:

    CC          = cc
    CFLAGS     := -Wall -O2 -fPIC \
                  $(shell pkg-config --cflags gtk+-3.0 \
                                              json-glib-1.0 \
                                              gmodule-2.0 \
                                              pipnode 2>/dev/null)
    LDLIBS     := $(shell pkg-config --libs gtk+-3.0 \
                                            json-glib-1.0 \
                                            gmodule-2.0 \
                                            pipnode 2>/dev/null)

    my_echo.so: my-echo.c
            $(CC) $(CFLAGS) -shared -o $@ $< $(LDLIBS)

    install: my_echo.so
            install -D -m 0644 my_echo.so \
                    $(DESTDIR)$(HOME)/.local/share/pipnode/plugins/my_echo.so

The `pkg-config --cflags pipnode` invocation resolves once
pipnode has been `make install`-ed: the host's top-level
`Makefile.am` installs `pipnode.pc` into
`$(libdir)/pkgconfig/`, and `pkg-config` picks it up from there
without any further configuration.  In addition to the usual
`Cflags:` / `Libs:` / `Requires:` fields, the .pc file exposes
two pkg-config variables a plugin's build system can query to
avoid hard-coding install paths:

  - `plugindir` -- the directory the host scans for plugin .so
    files (typically `$libdir/pipnode/plugins`).  Plugin
    Makefile.am rules should install into this directory so the
    default plugin search path (section 7) picks them up.

  - `helpdir`   -- the flat directory the host's help browser
    resolves `<TypeName>.html` files from (typically
    `$pkgdatadir/help`).  Plugin Makefile.am rules that ship
    per-node HTML help files install into this directory.

Both variables come out of `pkg-config --variable=plugindir
pipnode` and `pkg-config --variable=helpdir pipnode`
respectively.

Three filename conventions are worth knowing:

  - The shared object's basename is what the user sees in error
    messages and what pipnode uses for filename-based dedup
    (section 9).  Pick something readable, e.g. `my_echo.so`,
    `acme_crypto.so`.

  - There is no `lib` prefix.  Plugins are loadable modules,
    not libraries you link against, so the `lib` prefix that
    `gcc -shared` uses by default would only confuse readers.
    With autotools, `-module -avoid-version` produces the right
    name; with a hand-written Makefile, just call your output
    file `my_echo.so` directly.

  - Avoid SONAME version suffixes (`.so.0.0.0` and friends).
    `g_module_open` will only find `my_echo.so`, not
    `my_echo.so.0`.


6. BUILDING THE PLUGIN: AUTOTOOLS (THE BUNDLED APPROACH)
--------------------------------------------------------

The bundled `plugins/network/` plugin in this source tree is
built with autotools and libtool.  The relevant excerpts from
`plugins/network/Makefile.am`, suitable as a template for any
new bundled plugin or for an out-of-tree autotools project:

    plugindir = $(pkglibdir)/plugins

    plugin_LTLIBRARIES = my_echo.la

    my_echo_la_SOURCES = my-echo.c

    my_echo_la_CFLAGS  = $(GTK_CFLAGS) \
                         $(JSON_GLIB_CFLAGS) \
                         $(GMODULE_CFLAGS) \
                         -I$(top_srcdir)/lib

    my_echo_la_LDFLAGS = -module -avoid-version -shared

    my_echo_la_LIBADD  = $(top_builddir)/lib/libpipnode.la \
                         $(GTK_LIBS) \
                         $(JSON_GLIB_LIBS) \
                         $(GMODULE_LIBS)

What each piece is doing:

  - `plugindir = $(pkglibdir)/plugins` declares the install
    directory.  `pkglibdir` is `$prefix/lib/pipnode` by default,
    so the plugin lands at `/usr/local/lib/pipnode/plugins/` on
    a typical install.  This is exactly the directory pipnode
    scans at start-up (section 7).

  - `plugin_LTLIBRARIES` (rather than `lib_LTLIBRARIES`) tells
    automake to install into `$plugindir`.

  - `-module` tells libtool that the result is a loadable module
    (no `lib` prefix, no requirement that every symbol be
    resolvable at link time).

  - `-avoid-version` drops the SONAME `.0.0.0` suffix so the
    installed file is a plain `my_echo.so`.

  - `-shared` forces the shared form even on platforms where
    libtool would otherwise also emit a static archive.

  - `LIBADD` against `libpipnode.la` makes the plugin link
    against the host library so symbols like
    `pn_node_factory_register` and `pn_node_emit_message`
    resolve at run time against pipnode itself rather than
    being inlined into the .so.

For a plugin that lives outside this source tree, replace
`-I$(top_srcdir)/lib` with `$(PIPNODE_CFLAGS)` and the libtool
`LIBADD` line with `$(PIPNODE_LIBS)`, both produced by
`PKG_CHECK_MODULES([PIPNODE], [pipnode])` in your own
`configure.ac`.

A small autoconf helper, `PIPNODE_PLUGIN_CHECK`, is installed
alongside `pipnode.pc` (into `$(datadir)/aclocal/`) so an
out-of-tree plugin's `configure.ac` can do the whole probe in
one line:

    PIPNODE_PLUGIN_CHECK([0.1.0])  dnl minimum pipnode version

In addition to running the equivalent `PKG_CHECK_MODULES` and
`AC_SUBST`-ing `PIPNODE_CFLAGS` / `PIPNODE_LIBS`, the macro also
pulls `plugindir` and `helpdir` out of `pipnode.pc` and exposes
them as `PIPNODE_PLUGINDIR` and `PIPNODE_HELPDIR` so the
plugin's `Makefile.am` can install into the directories the
host actually scans at run-time without hard-coding
`$(libdir)/pipnode/plugins` or `$(datadir)/pipnode/help`:

    plugindir   = $(PIPNODE_PLUGINDIR)
    plugin_LTLIBRARIES = my_echo.la
    ...

    pnhelpdir   = $(PIPNODE_HELPDIR)
    pnhelp_DATA = help/MyEcho.html

The macro requires that the system pipnode install is recent
enough to ship `pipnode.pc` -- the macro errors out with a
clear message if the .pc file is present but does not expose a
`plugindir` variable, which would mean pipnode predates the
helper-file work.


7. WHERE PIPNODE LOOKS FOR PLUGINS
-----------------------------------

At start-up both `pipnode` (the GUI) and `pipnode-run` (the
headless runner) call `pn_node_factory_load_plugins_default()`,
which scans the following directories in order.  The first
plugin to claim a given basename wins (section 9), so paths
listed earlier override paths listed later.

  1. Every directory in the `PIPNODE_PLUGIN_PATH` environment
     variable.  Colon-separated on POSIX; pipnode honours the
     platform's native search-path separator
     (`G_SEARCHPATH_SEPARATOR_S`).

         PIPNODE_PLUGIN_PATH=/path/one:/path/two pipnode

     Useful for testing an in-development plugin against an
     installed pipnode without overwriting the installed copy.

  2. `$XDG_DATA_HOME/pipnode/plugins/`, with the canonical
     XDG fallback to `$HOME/.local/share/pipnode/plugins/`.
     The right place for a per-user install: drop a `.so` in
     here and the next pipnode start-up picks it up.

  3. `$pkglibdir/plugins/`, baked into pipnode at compile time
     via the `-DPKGLIBDIR=` macro.  Typically
     `/usr/local/lib/pipnode/plugins/` for a `./configure` with
     no `--prefix` override, or
     `/usr/lib/pipnode/plugins/` for a distribution package.
     This is where `make install` lands plugins built with the
     bundled-approach autotools rules from section 6.

  4. `<top_builddir>/plugins/*/.libs/`, a build-tree fallback
     used during development.  When pipnode is run straight
     from the source tree (without `make install`), this is
     how the bundled `pipnode-network` plugin becomes visible
     to `src/pipnode` and `src/pipnode-run` without anyone
     having to set `PIPNODE_PLUGIN_PATH`.  An installed
     pipnode also has this directory baked into the binary,
     but the path almost certainly does not exist on the
     target machine, so the loader silently skips it.

A directory that does not exist (or that cannot be opened) is
treated as "no plugins here" and produces no warning.  An
unreadable plugin file inside an existing directory does
produce a warning but does not abort the rest of the scan.

The GUI editor accepts `--no-plugins` to skip the scan
entirely for one session — no directory is searched and no
`.so` (logic or companion) is dlopened, so the palette shows
the built-in nodes only.  It is the quickest way to tell
whether a problem comes from a plugin, and a reliable escape
hatch when a stale or broken plugin would otherwise hang the
editor on the dlopen scan.  The flag is process-wide and
one-shot: it leaves the per-plugin opt-outs in Preferences
untouched, so the next ordinary start-up loads plugins as
before.  `pipnode-run` has no such flag — it always loads the
plugins a worksheet needs.


8. HOW A PLUGIN IS LOADED AT RUN TIME
-------------------------------------

For each candidate `.so` file found in the search path, the
loader does the following in `pn_node_factory_load_plugin()`:

  1.  Canonicalises the path with `g_canonicalize_filename`
      (resolves symlinks and `..` segments) so two paths that
      reach the same file dedupe against each other.

  2.  Checks that neither the canonical path nor the file's
      basename has been seen before; if it has, returns success
      silently without re-opening the file.

  3.  Calls `g_module_open(path, G_MODULE_BIND_LAZY |
      G_MODULE_BIND_LOCAL)`.  Lazy binding means symbols inside
      the plugin are resolved on first use rather than on load,
      which is faster.  Local binding keeps the plugin's
      symbols out of the global symbol table -- two plugins can
      both define a static helper called `read_byte` without
      the second one's call accidentally jumping into the first
      one's copy.

  4.  Calls `g_module_symbol(mod, "pn_plugin_init", &fn)`.
      A missing symbol fails the load with
      `PN_NODE_FACTORY_PLUGIN_ERROR_NO_ENTRY`.

  5.  Calls `fn(factory)`.  This is where the plugin runs its
      `G_DEFINE_TYPE`-generated `_get_type` accessors and its
      `pn_node_factory_register` calls.

  6.  Validates the returned `PnPluginInfo`: a NULL return is a
      failed load; an `abi_version` mismatch is a failed load
      and the types the plugin already registered are popped
      back off the factory; an empty `name` is a failed load.

  7.  Backfills `PnPluginInfo.name` onto every type the plugin
      registered whose `PnNodeClass.plugin_name` still reads
      the inherited `"Internal"` default.  This is how the
      inspector ends up showing your plugin's identifier as
      the provenance for nodes that did not bother overriding
      `plugin_name` themselves.

  8.  Calls `g_module_make_resident` so the .so cannot be
      unmapped, and adds both the canonical path and the
      basename to the dedup tables.

  9.  Logs `pipnode: loaded plugin "<name>" <version> from
      <path>` at the GLib `INFO` level.


9. DEDUPLICATION: WHAT STOPS A PLUGIN FROM LOADING TWICE
--------------------------------------------------------

The loader maintains two hash sets, both keyed by string:

  - `loaded_paths`     -- canonical `g_canonicalize_filename`
                         paths.  Catches the same physical file
                         reached through two overlapping search
                         directories (e.g. a symlink farm or
                         two directories listed in
                         `PIPNODE_PLUGIN_PATH`).

  - `loaded_basenames` -- `g_path_get_basename` of the same
                         paths.  Catches the case where two
                         physically distinct files ship the
                         same `GType` set: typically the
                         system-installed
                         `/usr/local/lib/pipnode/plugins/foo.so`
                         and the build-tree-fallback
                         `<top_builddir>/plugins/foo/.libs/foo.so`
                         are different inodes but register
                         identical types.

Without basename dedup the second registration would crash
with `cannot register existing type` because `GType` is a
process-global namespace.  Whichever directory was scanned
first wins; the search order above (env var, user, system,
build tree) is therefore also the precedence order for
overlapping plugins of the same name.


10. ABI VERSIONING
------------------

`PN_PLUGIN_ABI_VERSION` is a single integer in
`<pn-plugin.h>`.  It is bumped whenever an incompatible change
to `PnNode`, `PnNodeClass`, the factory registration contract,
or the plugin entry-point signature ships in a release.

A plugin built against ABI version N exposes "N" in its
`PnPluginInfo.abi_version`.  The host compares this to its own
`PN_PLUGIN_ABI_VERSION` and refuses to load on mismatch -- it
logs a warning and skips the plugin instead of letting a stale
.so in the user's plugin directory crash the process with a
silent layout mismatch.

What this means in practice:

  - Plugins are recompiled and redistributed against the new
    ABI when pipnode bumps it.  There is no compatibility
    shim layer.

  - A plugin that only ever needs to ship one node type and
    that uses no internal pipnode APIs is unlikely to be
    affected by ABI bumps in practice; the contract surface
    that the version protects is small.

  - If you maintain a plugin out-of-tree, treat the ABI
    version as a fast-failing match against pipnode's release
    notes: bump your plugin's version, recompile against the
    new headers, and ship.


11. INSPECTOR ATTRIBUTION
-------------------------

Every `PnNode` carries a `plugin_name` field on its class
struct, default `"Internal"`.  The factory shows this string
in the inspector pane and in the palette tooltip so the user
can tell where a given node type came from.

You can pin the value yourself in `_class_init`:

    nc->plugin_name = "EchoPlugin";

but you do not have to.  The loader backfills the value from
your `PnPluginInfo.name` for every type whose
`plugin_name` still equals the inherited `"Internal"` default,
so the common case (one plugin, several types, all attributed
to the same plugin) does not require a per-class override.


12. LIMITATIONS AND GOTCHAS
---------------------------

- *No unload.*  Once loaded, a plugin's .so stays mapped until
  the process exits.  Reloading a plugin during development
  requires restarting pipnode.

- *No partial loads.*  If your `pn_plugin_init` calls
  `pn_node_factory_register` ten times and then returns NULL,
  the loader pops the ten registrations off the factory but
  the underlying `GType` registrations stay in place -- they
  cannot be undone.  In practice this means you should validate
  whatever conditions your plugin needs (configuration files,
  external libraries) before the first
  `pn_node_factory_register`, not after.

- *GType names must be globally unique.*  Two plugins that both
  define a `MyEcho` GType cannot coexist in the same process;
  the second one's `g_type_register_static` will fail at the
  GLib level with `cannot register existing type`.  Pick a
  prefix that is unlikely to collide (e.g. your project's name
  or a registered DNS-style identifier).

- *Threading.*  Pipnode runs the GTK main loop on a single
  thread; node `receive` callbacks run on that thread.  If
  your plugin does anything blocking (network I/O, file
  system, `usleep`) it must do so from a worker thread and
  marshal results back to the main loop with
  `g_main_context_invoke`.  Look at how `PnPing` uses the
  worker-thread idiom for an idiomatic pattern.

- *Headless safety.*  `pipnode-run` does not link or
  initialise GTK widgets, but it does link `libpipnode` (which
  in turn is built against GTK).  Your plugin can safely call
  any GLib / GObject / GIO API in its node implementation; it
  must not call into `gtk_*` from inside `receive` or any
  other code path that runs under `pipnode-run`, since no GTK
  display has been opened.  Settings dialogs and other UI
  hooks must be guarded so they only run inside the GUI host.

- *Error reporting goes through the outbound message, not
  stdout/stderr.*  Pipnode is usually launched from the desktop
  (a `.desktop` launcher, an XFCE panel applet, a Flatpak), so
  the user never sees `g_warning`, `g_message`, `printf`, or
  any other text written to stdout/stderr -- those streams are
  attached to a journal the user does not read.  The primary
  failure-reporting channel is the node's outgoing message:
  set `success = FALSE` and put a one-line human-readable
  reason in the `output` member.  A downstream Debug or Text
  View node will then surface the message on the canvas where
  the user is looking.  See the Weather node for an example
  that classifies its failures (curl spawn failure, HTTP 4xx
  with the provider's reason, rate-limit fallback, empty body,
  non-JSON body) into specific `output` strings.  Reserve
  `g_warning` and friends for developer diagnostics that help
  someone running pipnode from a terminal track down a bug;
  they are not a substitute for an emitted failure message.
  The full message contract (`success`, `output`, `value`, the
  standard data-bag members) is documented in the HTML user
  manual at `data/help-index.html`.

- *Visual error state on the canvas: `pn_node_set_has_error`.*
  The emitted failure message above is the TEXTUAL channel; the
  matching VISUAL channel is a generic error flag every `PnNode`
  carries:

      pn_node_set_has_error (PN_NODE (self), TRUE);   /* broken   */
      pn_node_set_has_error (PN_NODE (self), FALSE);  /* recovered */

  While the flag is set the worksheet paints the node body red
  with a ❗ glyph, overriding the node's own colour/icon -- exactly
  the way it greys a `disabled` node.  The rendering is centralized
  in the host, so any node gets the indication for free: do NOT
  hand-roll your own "red body + warning icon" swap with
  `pn_node_set_color` / `pn_node_set_icon` (older nodes that still
  do are being migrated away from it).  Keep your node's normal
  (healthy) colour and icon set unconditionally and just toggle
  `has-error`; the host draws the overlay on top.

  The flag is transient runtime state: it is never written to the
  save file and never appears in the settings dialog, so it is
  cheap to flip from your connect / disconnect / operation-failed
  paths.  It needs no ABI bump -- it is a plain function on the
  base class, available to every plugin.

  This is the ONLY user-facing failure channel a sink-style node
  has: a node with `has_output = FALSE` cannot emit a downstream
  message, so the red ❗ is how it tells the user it is failing.
  The MQTT Source and Sink (`lib/pn-mqtt.c`, `lib/pn-mqtt-sink.c`)
  are the worked examples -- both keep their green identity and
  toggle `has-error` from a single `apply_visual_state` helper.

- *Per-node diagnostics log: `pn_node_log`.*  Every `PnNode`
  carries an in-memory log ring, and the right-click menu has a
  "Log..." entry (just under "Configure...") that opens a live
  per-node console showing it.  This is the THIRD failure channel,
  complementing the outbound failure message (textual, downstream)
  and `has-error` (visual, on-canvas): it is where a node records
  the running narrative a curious user would want -- what it is
  doing, and the detail behind a failure -- instead of writing to
  stdout/stderr, which the desktop-launched user never sees.

      pn_node_log_info    (PN_NODE (self), "connected to %s", url);
      pn_node_log_warning (PN_NODE (self), "retry %d", n);
      pn_node_log_error   (PN_NODE (self), "connect refused: %s", why);

  (or `pn_node_log (node, PN_LOG_LEVEL_*, fmt, ...)` for a computed
  level).  The message is a `printf`-style format; the entry is
  timestamped, kept oldest-first, and the ring caps at a couple
  hundred lines (oldest dropped).  Adding an entry emits
  `PnNode::log-changed`, so an open Log dialog updates live.  The
  storage is GTK-free and lives in `libpipnode-core`, so it is
  available under `pipnode-run` as well; only the dialog is GUI.

  Threading: like `receive` and `pn_node_emit_message`, call
  `pn_node_log` on the main loop thread.  A node that does blocking
  work on a worker thread should log from the same main-thread
  marshal point it already uses to deliver results (see the
  `g_main_context_invoke` idiom in the *Threading* bullet above) --
  do not call it directly from a libmosquitto / GTask worker
  callback.  The Notify node (`lib/pn-notify.c`) is the worked
  example: it logs an INFO line per notification it dispatches and
  a WARNING/ERROR line from its main-thread D-Bus paths.

  `pn_node_log` does NOT echo to stdout/stderr.  Reserve `g_warning`
  and friends for the developer-only diagnostics described above;
  use `pn_node_log` for anything you want the user to be able to
  read in the app.


13. DEBUGGING A PLUGIN THAT DOES NOT LOAD
-----------------------------------------

Start the binary with `G_MESSAGES_DEBUG=all` to see every
message the loader emits:

    G_MESSAGES_DEBUG=all pipnode

Then read the output for one of the four common failures.

(a) "failed to open plugin ...":
    `g_module_open` returned NULL.  The reason follows the
    colon -- usually a missing symbol from a transitive
    dependency.  Check with `ldd my_plugin.so` that every
    NEEDED entry resolves on the target system.

(b) "plugin ... does not export pn_plugin_init":
    The .so loaded but the entry-point symbol is absent or
    not visible.  Either you forgot the function, you spelled
    it wrong, or you forgot `G_MODULE_EXPORT` on a non-ELF
    toolchain.  `nm -D my_plugin.so | grep pn_plugin_init`
    should show a `T` entry; if it shows nothing or a `t` the
    symbol is private.

(c) "ABI version N does not match host ABI M":
    The .so was built against a different pipnode release.
    Recompile against the running pipnode's headers.

(d) Silent skip with no message:
    The directory the plugin lives in is not on the search
    path.  Confirm with `pn_node_factory_load_plugin` called
    explicitly (e.g. through `PIPNODE_PLUGIN_PATH=$PWD`) that
    the file is loadable in isolation.

If `pn_plugin_init` runs but no node appears in the palette,
double-check that:

  - your `_class_init` set both `category` and `class_name`
    (a NULL `class_name` is what makes the palette skip a
    type), and
  - your `pn_node_factory_register` call succeeded -- the
    function returns FALSE if the type is not a `PnNode`
    descendant or has already been registered.


14. SHIPPING PER-NODE HELP PAGES
--------------------------------

Pipnode's help browser opens a per-node HTML file when the user
right-clicks a palette entry and picks "Help".  The file is named
after the GType name -- for example `PnRtc.html` -- and lives in
the same flat $pkgdatadir/help/ directory whether it ships from the
host (libpipnode) or from a plugin.  The host's
resolver searches several directories in order:

  1. $PIPNODE_HELP_PATH                 (colon-separated dev override)
  2. $XDG_DATA_HOME/pipnode/help/       (per-user installs)
  3. $SRCDIR/data/help/                 (in-tree, host's own files)
  4. $SRCDIR/plugins/<x>/help/          (in-tree, bundled plugins)
  5. $PKGDATADIR/help/                  (system install)

To ship a help file with your plugin:

  - Drop one HTML file per node into your plugin's `help/`
    directory; name each file exactly `<TypeName>.html` (the
    GType name, including the `Pn` prefix).

  - Reference the shared stylesheet with a sibling href --
    `<link rel="stylesheet" href="help.css">` -- so the file
    inherits the host's typography without carrying its own
    copy.  The host installs `help.css` into the same
    directory as your file at install time.

  - Optionally cross-link back to the host index with
    `<a href="help-index.html">` so the user can jump up to
    the table of contents.

  - In your plugin's Makefile.am, ship the files with:

        pnhelpdir = $(pkgdatadir)/help
        pnhelp_DATA = \
            help/<TypeNameA>.html \
            help/<TypeNameB>.html
        EXTRA_DIST = $(pnhelp_DATA)

    and they install alongside the host's help directory.

  - For dev / test runs against the build tree, no extra
    plumbing is needed: the in-tree fallback walks every
    `plugins/<x>/help/` so `make` followed by `src/pipnode`
    finds your help files without `make install`.

A plugin that omits the help file falls back gracefully: the
right-click "Help" entry opens the host's help-index.html
instead of leaving the menu item silent.

The bundled `plugins/network/help/` (PnPing, PnDns) is a worked
example of the shape your own plugin's help/ directory should
take.


15. EXTENDING THE NODE SETTINGS DIALOG (ABI v2)
-----------------------------------------------

The auto-generated property tab the dialog draws for every class in
the node's GObject type chain is enough for any node that is fully
described by plain GObject properties: a string editor for strings,
a check button for booleans, an enum combo for enums, and so on.
Plugin-shipped node types get this for free -- no extra work in the
plugin's `_class_init` is required.

Three things, however, the auto-generated tab cannot do on its own,
and they are the reason for the dialog-extension hooks introduced
in ABI v2:

  - The right widget for one of your properties is not the default.
    Examples: a serial-port path that should pick from a sysfs walk
    instead of accepting any string; a file path that should open a
    file chooser; a numeric field whose valid range depends on
    another property; a read-only label for a value the node writes
    itself (a fetched rate, a last-update timestamp).

  - Your configuration is structured rather than scalar -- a list
    of rules, an array of {key, value} pairs, a route table.  The
    auto-generated tab would render the underlying JSON-string
    property as a single giant entry, which is unusable.

  - Configuration needs more than one tab -- a Diagnostics page
    showing live state, a Logs tail, a one-shot "Scan for devices"
    button.

Three optional vfunc slots on `PnNodeClass` cover these.  All three
default to `NULL`; assigning any one of them is opt-in.  The dialog
calls them once per dialog open with the node instance, the
specific GParamSpec (for `build_property_editor`), and the
PnNodeDialog GtkWindow (suitable as the `transient-for` of any
child dialog you spawn).

Helpers exposed for the rows / pages you do NOT want to write from
scratch live in `<pipnode/pn-node-dialog-helpers.h>`:

```c
GtkWidget *pn_node_dialog_new_property_grid (void);
void       pn_node_dialog_attach_row        (GtkGrid *grid, guint row,
                                             const gchar *label,
                                             GtkWidget *editor);
GtkWidget *pn_node_dialog_default_editor    (GObject *target,
                                             GParamSpec *pspec);
void       pn_node_dialog_append_page       (GtkNotebook *notebook,
                                             GtkWidget *page,
                                             const gchar *title);
```

`pn_node_dialog_default_editor` is the same builder the auto-
generated tab uses; calling it from your override delegates one
property back to the host without recursing into your override
again.

15.1 build_property_editor: take over one row
---------------------------------------------

```c
GtkWidget *(*build_property_editor) (PnNode      *self,
                                     GParamSpec  *pspec,
                                     GObject     *target,
                                     GtkWindow   *dialog_parent);
```

Called once for every writable property the dialog renders for
this class.  Return a #GtkWidget bound to @target via the standard
GObject machinery (`g_object_bind_property`,
`g_signal_connect_object`) to take the row over; return %NULL to
let the host's default editor build the row.  Use
`g_signal_connect_object (target, "notify::other_prop", cb, your_widget, 0)`
when the editor needs to react to changes on a sibling property
-- the handler auto-disconnects when the dialog closes and your
widget is destroyed.

Worked example -- a serial-port combo populated from a live
sysfs walk:

```c
static GtkWidget *
my_node_build_property_editor (PnNode      *self,
                               GParamSpec  *pspec,
                               GObject     *target,
                               GtkWindow   *parent)
{
    if (g_strcmp0 (pspec->name, "device") != 0)
        return NULL;                                /* host default */

    GtkWidget *combo = gtk_combo_box_text_new ();
    gchar **devices = my_node_list_devices ();      /* sysfs walk */

    gtk_combo_box_text_append (GTK_COMBO_BOX_TEXT (combo),
                               "", "No Device");
    for (gchar **p = devices; p && *p; p++)
        gtk_combo_box_text_append (GTK_COMBO_BOX_TEXT (combo),
                                   *p, *p);
    g_strfreev (devices);

    gtk_widget_set_hexpand (combo, TRUE);
    g_object_bind_property (target, "device", combo, "active-id",
                            G_BINDING_SYNC_CREATE
                            | G_BINDING_BIDIRECTIONAL);
    return combo;
}
```

Wire it up in `_class_init`:

```c
node_class->build_property_editor = my_node_build_property_editor;
```

15.2 build_class_tab: replace the whole property tab
----------------------------------------------------

```c
GtkWidget *(*build_class_tab) (PnNode    *self,
                               GtkWindow *dialog_parent);
```

Called once instead of the introspection-driven default.  Return
the #GtkWidget that should sit in this class's notebook tab;
return %NULL to fall back to the default.  Use this when the
configuration is a list / table / graph editor that does not
naturally decompose into one widget per GObject property -- the
in-tree #PnFilter and #PnSet rule editors are the worked
examples.  When you want the host's grid layout for some rows
and your own widget for others, build a
`pn_node_dialog_new_property_grid()` and attach via
`pn_node_dialog_attach_row()` so your tab matches the look of
its siblings.

15.3 build_extra_pages: append additional notebook pages
--------------------------------------------------------

```c
void (*build_extra_pages) (PnNode      *self,
                           GtkNotebook *notebook,
                           GtkWindow   *dialog_parent);
```

Called once after the per-class property tabs are populated,
walked in chain order so the leaf class's pages land last.
Implementations call `pn_node_dialog_append_page (notebook, page,
"Title")` for each extra page.  Use for content that is not
naturally a property form: a Diagnostics page showing live signal
strength, a Logs tail, a "Scan for devices" button.

15.4 The free `busy` overlay
----------------------------

If your node exposes a read-only boolean property named `busy`,
the dialog will automatically:

  - watch it via `notify::busy`;
  - centre a spinner with the label "Initializing device…" over
    the entire notebook while the property is `TRUE`;
  - lock every page in the notebook from interaction during that
    window.

This is implemented in the host with `g_object_class_find_property
(klass, "busy")` -- no opt-in vfunc, no helper to call.  Plugin
node types that need to lock their settings dialog while a worker
thread runs an async device handshake just need to install the
property; everything else falls into place.

15.5 ABI version requirement
----------------------------

Using any of the three vfuncs requires `PN_PLUGIN_ABI_VERSION = 2`.
A plugin built against ABI v1 can keep loading against an ABI v2
host (the host's loader matches on the version field), but the
v1 plugin's `PnNodeClass` struct does not carry the new vfunc
slots so the dialog falls back to the host default for every
property -- exactly the v1 behaviour.  Plugins compiled against
ABI v2 refuse to load against an ABI v1 host (the version check
catches the mismatch up-front and surfaces a clear `GError`).


16. DECLARATIVE SETTINGS SCHEMA (THE GTK-FREE DEFAULT PATH)
----------------------------------------------------------

The dialog hooks in section 15 are imperative: they hand you a
`GtkNotebook` / `GtkWidget` and you build the UI in C against GTK.
That is exactly what makes a plugin that uses them pull the GTK tier
into every host -- including a headless server (the problem section 17
solves).  But the great majority of real dialog customisations are not
"build me an arbitrary widget tree"; they are small, declarable tweaks:
group properties into tabs, force a particular editor for one property,
offer a fixed choice list, grey a row out unless another property has a
certain value.  None of that needs GTK at author time -- only data.

A `PnSettingsSchema` is that data: a GTK-free, declarative description
of a node type's dialog, built imperatively in `_class_init` and
attached to the class.  The headless core only ever holds the
description; the GUI tier turns it into GtkWidgets when the editor opens
the dialog.  A node or plugin whose dialog needs are covered by the
schema therefore stays entirely in the core tier -- ONE GTK-free `.so`,
no `-gui.so` companion (section 17), and it still shows a fully
customised dialog in the editor.  This is the "default path" of the
headless split; the companion is the escape hatch for what the schema
cannot express.

The schema is stored as per-`GType` qdata, so attaching one changes
neither the `PnNodeClass` struct layout nor the plugin ABI.  The header
is `<pipnode/pn-settings-schema.h>`.

16.1 The builder API
--------------------

```c
PnSettingsSchema *pn_settings_schema_new   (void);

/* Open a named tab; later rows attach to it.  Naming >= 1 tab REPLACES
 * the class's single auto property tab with one page per named tab
 * (the declarative counterpart of build_class_tabs).  Name no tab and
 * the single auto tab is kept and you only customise individual rows. */
void pn_settings_schema_tab (PnSettingsSchema *self, const gchar *title);

/* Append a row for a property.  PN_EDITOR_AUTO infers the editor from
 * the property type (right for plain tab grouping); the other kinds
 * force one: ENTRY, MULTILINE, SPIN, CHECK, COMBO, COLOR, FILE, CODE
 * (GtkSourceView), LABEL (read-only).  A prop that resolves to nothing
 * writable is silently skipped at render time. */
void pn_settings_schema_row (PnSettingsSchema *self,
                             const gchar *prop, PnEditorKind kind);

/* Attach an explicit, non-enum choice list to a row and force a combo
 * (for strings, each entry is both the label and the stored value). */
void pn_settings_schema_choices (PnSettingsSchema *self,
                                 const gchar *prop,
                                 const gchar *const *choices);

/* Per-row layout / visibility: PN_ROW_FLAG_FULL_WIDTH (span both columns,
 * no label cell), PN_ROW_FLAG_READONLY (always disabled), and
 * PN_ROW_FLAG_HIDDEN (drop the row from the dialog entirely -- see 16.3). */
void pn_settings_schema_row_flags (PnSettingsSchema *self,
                                   const gchar *prop, PnRowFlags flags);

/* Sensitivity dependencies: enable a row only while a controller
 * property equals a value (parsed against the controller's type -- an
 * enum by nick/name, a bool by true/false/1/0, a number by value) ... */
void pn_settings_schema_enable_when_eq (PnSettingsSchema *self,
                                        const gchar *prop,
                                        const gchar *when_prop,
                                        const gchar *value);
/* ... or only while a controller is "truthy" (nonzero / TRUE / non-empty). */
void pn_settings_schema_enable_when_truthy (PnSettingsSchema *self,
                                            const gchar *prop,
                                            const gchar *when_prop);

/* Attach the finished schema to the class; takes the caller's ref. */
void pn_node_class_set_settings_schema (PnNodeClass *klass,
                                        PnSettingsSchema *schema);
```

16.2 Worked example -- one greyed-out row, no tabs
--------------------------------------------------

The in-tree LED node's `hold-ms` field only does anything in Flash
mode, so it is greyed out otherwise.  This used to be hand-wired in a
GTK `build_property_editor`; as a schema it needs no GTK, so LED's
drawing companion shrinks and the logic half carries the whole dialog
description:

```c
/* in pn_led_class_init, with klass the PnLedClass */
PnSettingsSchema *schema = pn_settings_schema_new ();

pn_settings_schema_row (schema, "hold-ms", PN_EDITOR_AUTO);
pn_settings_schema_enable_when_eq (schema, "hold-ms",
                                   "mode", "Flash");   /* PnLedMode nick */

pn_node_class_set_settings_schema (PN_NODE_CLASS (klass), schema);
```

No tab is named, so this keeps LED's single auto tab and only adjusts
the one row.  Name tabs instead when you want to group properties:

```c
PnSettingsSchema *schema = pn_settings_schema_new ();

pn_settings_schema_tab (schema, "General");
pn_settings_schema_row (schema, "label",   PN_EDITOR_AUTO);
pn_settings_schema_row (schema, "mode",    PN_EDITOR_AUTO);

pn_settings_schema_tab (schema, "Appearance");
pn_settings_schema_row (schema, "color",   PN_EDITOR_COLOR);
pn_settings_schema_row (schema, "size",    PN_EDITOR_SPIN);

pn_node_class_set_settings_schema (PN_NODE_CLASS (klass), schema);
```

16.3 Hiding a property row -- including an inherited one
-------------------------------------------------------

`PN_ROW_FLAG_HIDDEN` drops a property row from the dialog completely.
Unlike `PN_ROW_FLAG_READONLY` (which shows the row greyed out) the row
is never built at all.  Tag it like any other row flag:

```c
pn_settings_schema_row_flags (schema, "topic", PN_ROW_FLAG_HIDDEN);
```

The flag works for ANY property, including one a PARENT class declared.
Row visibility is resolved by walking the node's class chain from the
leaf down to the class that declared the property, and the NEAREST
schema that names the row wins.  So a base class can ship a property
and a subclass can hide it; conversely a base class can hide a row by
default and a subclass can force it back by naming the SAME row with no
`PN_ROW_FLAG_HIDDEN` bit (`PN_ROW_FLAG_NONE` re-shows it).  This is how
a plugin that subclasses one of the base classes in sections 19 / 20
tailors the inherited rows without touching the base class.

The base `topic` property is the worked example.  A topic template is
only meaningful for nodes that EMIT a message stamped with it, so a
node that never does -- a sink, or a display that only consumes its
input -- hides the row explicitly:

```c
PnSettingsSchema *schema = pn_settings_schema_new ();
pn_settings_schema_row       (schema, "topic", PN_EDITOR_AUTO);
pn_settings_schema_row_flags (schema, "topic", PN_ROW_FLAG_HIDDEN);
pn_node_class_set_settings_schema (PN_NODE_CLASS (klass), schema);
```

There is no automatic host default -- visibility is whatever the
schemas in the chain say -- so the decision is explicit and auditable
per type.  Tagging the row on a shared base class (the image-filter
base, an MQTT-sink base) hides it for every leaf at once; a leaf that
DOES want the Topic row back names a plain `topic` row (no HIDDEN) to
override the base.  The MQTT Sink is the canonical case: it republishes
on the incoming message's own topic, so it hides the base Topic row.

`PN_ROW_FLAG_HIDDEN` lives in qdata with the rest of the schema, so it
changes neither the `PnNodeClass` layout nor the plugin ABI (see 16.5).

16.4 When the schema is not enough
----------------------------------

The schema deliberately does NOT cover everything.  A combo whose
entries are discovered at runtime (a sysfs device walk, a `tmux
list-sessions`), a live status row that updates while the dialog is
open, a one-shot "Download" button with a progress label, a custom
list/table editor -- those need real code and so need either the
imperative vfuncs of section 15 (fine for a desktop-only plugin) or, to
stay server-installable, the companion module of section 17.  The two
paths compose: an imperative `build_*` vfunc on a class always WINS over
that class's schema, so you can describe the static rows as data and
override only the one dynamic row in a companion.

16.5 ABI note
-------------

The schema lives in qdata, not in the `PnNodeClass` struct, so using it
does not change the plugin ABI and needs no new `PN_PLUGIN_ABI_VERSION`.
A host too old to know about schemas simply ignores the qdata and draws
the auto dialog; a schema-aware host renders it.


17. COMPANION GUI MODULE: SERVER-INSTALLABLE PLUGINS (ABI v3)
------------------------------------------------------------

The dialog hooks in section 15 (and the cairo painter vfuncs
`paint_header_overlay` / `paint_plot` / `get_size`) all touch GTK
and cairo.  A plugin that sets them from `pn_plugin_init` therefore
has to link the GTK tier (`libpipnode-gui`), which means its single
`.so` pulls the whole GTK/cairo/pango stack into EVERY host that
loads it -- including the headless runner `pipnode-run` on a server
with no display.  That is fine for a desktop-only plugin but defeats
the point of the headless core.

Reach for this only when the declarative schema (section 16) cannot
express your dialog -- a runtime-discovered combo, a live status row, a
"Download" button, a custom table editor, or any cairo drawing.  If the
schema covers you, prefer it: one GTK-free `.so`, no companion.

ABI v3 lets you split a plugin the same way the in-tree dual-nature
nodes are split: logic in one GTK-free `.so`, drawing and dialogs in
a separate companion the editor loads and the server never does.

  - The LOGIC module `myplugin.so` links `libpipnode-core` ONLY.  It
    exports `pn_plugin_init`, registers the node types, and carries
    `receive()` plus any pure-data state.  It must NOT set the GTK
    vfuncs (`build_property_editor`, `build_class_tab`,
    `build_class_tabs`, `build_extra_pages`, `paint_*`) -- leave those
    slots NULL.  This is the only half a headless server installs;
    `pipnode-run` loads it and runs the worksheet with no GTK.

  - The COMPANION module `myplugin-gui.so` (the logic basename plus
    the `-gui` infix before the module suffix -- the constant
    `PN_PLUGIN_GUI_INFIX` in `pn-plugin.h`) links the GTK tier and
    exports a SECOND entry point:

        G_MODULE_EXPORT const PnPluginInfo *
        pn_plugin_gui_init (PnNodeFactory *factory);

    The editor calls it after the logic `.so` has registered the
    types; it installs the GTK vfunc slots onto the already-registered
    classes and returns a `PnPluginInfo` (same ABI check as
    `pn_plugin_init`).

How the host wires it up:

  - The CORE loader (`pipnode-run` AND the editor) discovers and loads
    `*.so` exactly as before, but SKIPS any file whose name ends in
    `-gui.<suffix>` -- a companion is never a standalone plugin.
  - The editor ALSO calls `pn_gui_load_plugin_companions()` after the
    plugin scan: for each logic plugin it loaded, it looks for the
    sibling `-gui.<suffix>` next to it and, if present, calls its
    `pn_plugin_gui_init()`.  A missing companion is not an error -- the
    node simply renders with the host's default painter and the
    auto-generated settings tab.
  - `pipnode-run` never looks for a companion, so the GTK half never
    loads on a server.

CRITICAL -- resolve your type by NAME in the companion:

    GType        t     = g_type_from_name ("MyGauge");
    PnNodeClass *klass = PN_NODE_CLASS (g_type_class_ref (t));
    klass->paint_header_overlay  = my_gauge_paint;
    klass->build_property_editor = my_gauge_build_editor;

The host loads the logic `.so` with `G_MODULE_BIND_LOCAL`, so its
symbols (the `my_gauge_get_type()` getter, any read-seam accessors)
are NOT visible to the separately-dlopened companion.  Calling the
logic header's `MY_TYPE_GAUGE` macro from the companion fails at load
time with `undefined symbol: my_gauge_get_type`.  `g_type_from_name()`
needs no symbol -- the type is already in the global GType registry
once the logic half ran.  Drive the node through public API only:
GObject properties (`g_object_get` / `g_object_bind_property`) and the
`PnNode` vfunc table.  If a painter needs state that is not a readable
property, expose it as one from the logic half (which also keeps the
headless side inspectable).

The minimal reference split lives in `tests/plugins/echo/`: `pn-echo.c`
(logic, core-only) plus `pn-echo-gui.c` (companion).  Its Makefile.am
shows the two `-module` builds, and the pair is verified by
`tests/test_plugin_load.py` (logic under `pipnode-run`, no GTK) and
`tests/test_plugin_gui_companion.py` (companion under the editor).

Two BUNDLED plugins ship as real two-tier examples (section 18):
`plugins/shell/` (the Tmux Monitor node's companion adds a live session
combobox + a runtime status row) and `plugins/sound-effects/` (the
SciFi Sound node's companion is a whole libsoup clip-download manager).
The latter also shows the common case where the two halves share a
GTK-free helper file (`pn-sci-fi-clips.c`) compiled into BOTH modules,
because the editor opens the companion `G_MODULE_BIND_LOCAL` and so a
logic-half symbol is invisible to it.


17a. WHEN A NODE STAYS A BUILT-IN INSTEAD
-----------------------------------------

The two-tier split pays off when the companion's dependency on the
logic half is *small and cleanly separable*: a read seam (one or two
GObject properties the painter / dialog reads through `g_object_get`),
a self-contained helper used only by the dialog (the Ollama node's
`/api/tags` query — `plugins/ollama/`), or a modest GTK-free helper the
two halves share by compiling it into both (`pn-sci-fi-clips.c`).  When
the dialog instead reuses a *large IO/protocol layer that is entangled
with the node's own runtime*, neither escape hatch is a good trade, and
the node is better left built into libpipnode with its dialog in the gui
tier (installed from `pn-gui.c` via the in-tree
`pn_<node>_gui_install()` idiom — the pre-plugin equivalent of a
companion).

The in-tree example is the **Meshtastic** node (`lib/pn-meshtastic.c` +
`lib/pn-meshtastic-gui.c`).  Its settings dialog does not just read
scalar state: the channel combo runs a live serial handshake against the
selected device to enumerate channels (`pn_meshtastic_list_channels` ->
`serial_open` + `run_handshake` + the `mesh_session` / protobuf layer,
~700 lines) and *also* reads the running worker's live channel cache.
That protocol layer is shared with the node's own worker thread, so a
clean companion split would force one of:

  - extracting the whole ~700-line GTK-free serial/protobuf layer into a
    shared module compiled into BOTH `.so` (the `pn-sci-fi-clips.c`
    pattern, but an order of magnitude larger and interleaved with node
    code), or

  - dropping the dialog's on-demand handshake and exposing only the
    cached channel list as a read-only property — a user-visible
    regression (you could no longer pick a channel on a node that has
    not connected yet).

The device-path combo, by contrast, is a self-contained sysfs walk
(`pn_meshtastic_list_devices`) that would move to a companion with no
trouble — but it is the cheap part.  Because the expensive part has no
clean seam and the only payoff is being a separately-installable `.so`,
Meshtastic stays a built-in.  Reach for a companion only when the seam
is cheap; when it is not, a built-in node with a gui-tier dialog is the
right shape.


17b. DEVICE-DIALOG TOOLKIT (DEVICES MENU + DIALOG SHELL)
-------------------------------------------------------

The settings dialog hooks above (sections 15-17) extend a *node's* own
dialog -- the one you reach by double-clicking the node on the sheet.
Some devices instead want a standalone configuration window reached from
the editor's **Devices** menu: scan for the hardware, pick one, walk a
notebook of settings pages, write them back.  The Meshtastic dialog is
the worked example.  The device-dialog toolkit is the public gui-tier
API a companion uses to build one of these without re-implementing the
frame, exactly as `pn-node-dialog-helpers.h` does for node dialogs.

It is three installed headers, all in `libpipnode-gui` (so a companion
already links them; a headless logic `.so` never sees them):

  - `pn-device-form.h` -- form helpers that fill a notebook tab with the
    house style: `pn_device_form_new_tab` / `_add_section` (an expander
    group), `_attach_label_row` (a "--"-fallback read-only value row),
    `_attach_entry_row` (entry + Apply), `_attach_control_row` (the
    region-cell + size-group idiom combos and spinners pack into),
    `_key_label`, `_set_value`, and the combo helpers `_combo_fill` /
    `_combo_select` / `_combo_get_id` (with `PnDeviceEnumEntry`).

  - `pn-device-dialog.h` -- the "dialog shell" object `PnDeviceDialog`:
    a modeless `GtkDialog` whose body is a `GtkNotebook` with a
    ref-counted busy spinner floated over it (`pn_device_dialog_push_busy`
    / `_pop_busy` / `_is_busy`) above a status bar (`_set_status` /
    `_set_statusf`), plus a Close button.  You POPULATE it
    (`_append_page`, `_set_pages_sensitive`) rather than subclass it.
    Pass `PN_DEVICE_DIALOG_WITH_DEVICE_LIST` to `_new` and the shell also
    builds the left-hand device-list pane (3-state empty/scanning/list
    stack + `GtkListBox`), driven through `_set_scan_callback` /
    `_set_selected_callback`, `_set_device_rows` (wholesale, with the
    reselect/auto-select policy) or the incremental `_add` / `_update` /
    `_remove_device_row`, `_set_list_hints`, `_set_auto_scan` and
    `_reselect_device`.  The handle is owned by the underlying dialog --
    never free it yourself.

  - `pn-device-provider.h` -- the **Devices-menu provider registry**.
    The menu is not hardcoded; it is built by enumerating this registry,
    so a device kind advertises itself by registering a provider:

        void pn_device_provider_register (const gchar *id,
                                          const gchar *display_name,
                                          const gchar *icon_name,
                                          PnDeviceProviderPresentFunc present,
                                          gpointer user_data,
                                          GDestroyNotify destroy);

    `id` is a stable machine key ("meshtastic"); the menu shows
    `display_name` + `icon_name`; activating the entry calls `present
    (parent, user_data)`, which opens (or raises) your dialog.
    Registering an `id` that already exists REPLACES it in place (its
    `destroy` runs), keeping menu order stable across a companion reload,
    so registration is idempotent.  `pn_device_provider_list()` returns a
    deep-copied snapshot of the registry in registration order;
    `pn_device_provider_present(id, parent)` fires a provider by id.  The
    registry is process-global, main-thread only, and has no unregister
    (providers are resident-lifetime, like the GUI modules that register
    them).

WHERE YOU REGISTER -- and why no ABI bump:

  - A BUILT-IN device kind registers from `pn_gui_install_builtin_nodes()`
    in `pn-gui.c` (Meshtastic does, via `pn_mesh_dialog_register_provider()`).
  - A PLUGIN registers from its companion's `pn_plugin_gui_init()`
    (section 17) -- the same entry point that installs node-dialog
    vfuncs.  The companion already links `libpipnode-gui`, so the toolkit
    symbols resolve directly; no `g_type_from_name` dance is needed
    because these are plain functions, not type-keyed vfunc slots.

The whole toolkit is *new exported gui-tier symbols*, not new
`PnNodeClass` vfuncs, so it does NOT bump `PN_PLUGIN_ABI_VERSION`: a
companion built against an older ABI keeps loading, and one that wants a
Devices entry simply calls these functions.  The Devices menu lives in
its own translation unit (`src/pn-devices-menu.c`) that enumerates the
registry, so adding a provider never touches `pn-window.c`.

Three scroll/edit-safe widgets the form helpers (and your own tabs)
build on are also public, in `pipnode.h`:

    PnDeviceCombo     (`pn_device_combo_new`)            a GtkComboBoxText
        that swallows the mouse wheel instead of stepping its selection
        -- the wheel scrolls the enclosing dialog, as a user expects.
    PnDeviceSpin      (`pn_device_spin_new_with_range`)  the same wheel-
        safe treatment for a GtkSpinButton.
    PnInlineEditLabel (`pn_inline_edit_label_new`)       a click-the-
        pencil-to-edit label (a GtkStack swapping a GtkLabel for a
        GtkEntry; GtkEditableLabel is GTK4-only).

WORKED EXAMPLE -- Meshtastic (`lib/pn-mesh-dialog.c`).  It builds its
window with `pn_device_dialog_new(..., PN_DEVICE_DIALOG_WITH_DEVICE_LIST)`,
wires the shell's scan callback to its sysfs walk and the selected
callback to a serial connect, fills each notebook tab with the
`pn_device_form_*` helpers, drives `push_busy` / `pop_busy` around the
3-second handshake, and advertises itself with
`pn_mesh_dialog_register_provider()` ->
`pn_device_provider_register("meshtastic", "Meshtastic",
"network-wireless", ...)`.  An out-of-tree device plugin follows the same
shape from its companion module.


18. HOST-PROVISIONED PROFILES: CREDENTIALS, SETTINGS, PERMISSIONS (ABI v5)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------

A plugin should not own the host, username, password, token, or
permission grant its nodes need to reach a service.  Those are
site-specific (a broker address belongs to the machine, not the plugin
source) and security-sensitive (a password must never land in a
shareable workflow file).  ABI v5 moves them to the *host*: the plugin
*declares* what a connection needs, the user *provisions* a named
instance once, and a node *references* it and *resolves* the values at
run time.

The three pieces:

  (a) A profile *type* (a #PnProfileSchema): a stable id, a display
      name, and an ordered list of typed fields.  One field *kind*
      unifies the three scopes the feature covers:

        PN_FIELD_STRING / _INT / _BOOL / _ENUM   plain settings
        PN_FIELD_SECRET                          a credential — masked
                                                 in the manager, kept in
                                                 the 0600 vault, never in
                                                 a workflow file
        PN_FIELD_PERMISSION                      a capability grant —
                                                 defaults off, the user
                                                 must explicitly enable it

      Build it in `pn_plugin_init` and register it with the factory,
      exactly the way you register node types:

      ```c
      PnProfileSchema *s = pn_profile_schema_new ("acme-cloud",
                                                  "Acme Cloud");
      pn_profile_schema_field (s, "url",      "URL",      PN_FIELD_STRING);
      pn_profile_schema_field (s, "api-key",  "API key",  PN_FIELD_SECRET);
      pn_profile_schema_field (s, "allow-writes",
                               "Allow writes", PN_FIELD_PERMISSION);
      pn_profile_schema_set_help_page (s, "AcmeCloudProfile.html");
      pn_node_factory_register_profile_type (factory, s); /* takes the ref */
      ```

      `pn_profile_schema_set_help_page` turns on a Help button on the
      type's page of the credentials manager: clicking it opens
      `AcmeCloudProfile.html` in the help browser.  This is where you
      explain to a non-developer end user how to install the remote
      subsystem (set up the broker, enable the device's API, &hellip;)
      and where to find the values that go in each field — the same
      naming convention and search path as per-node help pages: ship
      the HTML in your plugin's `help/` directory and add it to
      `pnhelp_DATA` in the plugin's `Makefile.am`.  Without a help page
      the button is hidden.

  (b) A node *references* a profile through an ordinary string property
      tagged in `_class_init` — the property stores only the chosen
      profile's non-secret *id* (which is what serialises into the
      workflow file):

      ```c
      props[PROP_SERVER] = g_param_spec_string (
              "server", "Server", "Acme Cloud profile.", "",
              G_PARAM_READWRITE | G_PARAM_STATIC_STRINGS);
      pn_param_spec_set_profile_ref (props[PROP_SERVER], "acme-cloud");
      ```

      The node settings dialog renders a tagged property as a profile
      picker (a combo of that type's profiles plus a leading "Default"
      entry that maps to the empty id) with an Edit button onto the
      credentials manager — no companion GUI module required, the host
      builds the picker.

  (c) The node *resolves* the values at FIRE time:

      ```c
      PnProfile *p = pn_node_get_profile (PN_NODE (self), "server");
      gchar *url = pn_profile_get_string (p, "url");      /* "" if unset */
      gchar *key = pn_profile_get_secret (p, "api-key");
      gboolean may_write = pn_profile_get_permission (p, "allow-writes");
      ```

      An empty reference follows the type's *primary* profile, so the
      common case (one server) needs zero per-node setup; a non-empty
      reference targets a specific profile, so two nodes can talk to two
      servers.  `pn_node_get_profile` returns %NULL when nothing is
      configured.

Where the values live.  The host stores provisioned instances in a
single JSON file in the user's config dir
(`$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/pipnode/credentials.json`), created and held at mode
0600, separate from both the workflow file and `preferences.json`.  A
node never writes there itself; the editor's credentials manager (Edit
-> Credentials) does.  Field resolution consults, in order:

  1. an environment override — `PIPNODE_PROFILE_<ID>_<FIELD>`, upper-
     cased with every non-alphanumeric byte folded to `_` (so a server
     or CI can inject a secret with no file at all);
  2. the value stored in the vault;
  3. the field's schema default.

Override the whole path with `PIPNODE_CREDENTIALS_FILE`.

Keeping legacy secrets out of the file.  A node that still carries an
inline secret property (for back-compat with pre-v5 files) tags it with
`pn_param_spec_set_secret()`; pn_flow's serialiser then *never* writes
it, so re-saving a migrated file scrubs the plaintext.  The bundled
network nodes also run a one-shot import in `constructed()` that lifts a
legacy inline username/password into a profile the first time an old
file is opened.

Live updates.  #PnVault emits a `changed` signal after every edit; a
node holding a live connection re-resolves on it (connect with
`g_signal_connect_object (pn_vault_get_default (), "changed", …)`), so
provisioning a profile takes effect immediately rather than only after
the node's own properties next change.

Tier placement.  #PnProfileSchema, #PnVault, the factory registration,
the profile-ref tag and `pn_node_get_profile` are all in the GTK-free
core, so headless `pipnode-run` resolves secrets from the file / env
with no display.  Only the credentials manager and the picker are
editor-side.

ABI version requirement.  Declaring a profile type uses the v5 factory
contract, so a plugin that calls `pn_node_factory_register_profile_type`
must declare `abi_version = PN_PLUGIN_ABI_VERSION` (>= 5); an older host
refuses it rather than silently registering nothing.  See
`plugins/network/` (the `mqtt-broker` and `http-basic` types, used by
the MQTT and HTTPS Tunnel nodes) and `tests/plugins/vaultecho/` (a
minimal type + referencing node) for worked examples.


19. WEBSOCKET SOURCE BASE CLASS WITH OPTIONAL PROFILE BINDING (ABI v6)
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Source nodes whose data feed arrives over a long-lived `ws://` or
`wss://` connection should derive from `#PnWebsocket` (in `<pipnode/pn-
ws.h>`) instead of rolling a SoupSession by hand.  The base class owns
the session and the live connection, drives an automatic reconnect loop
with exponential backoff, paints the "needs configuration" body state
when there is no URL yet, and dispatches inbound text frames to the
subclass on the main thread.

A subclass overrides three vfuncs:

      static gboolean my_is_configured (PnWebsocket *self);
      static void     my_connected     (PnWebsocket *self);
      static void     my_text_message  (PnWebsocket *self,
                                        const gchar *text);

`is_configured` gates the connect loop (default: %TRUE iff the inherited
`url` property is non-empty); `connected` is called once per successful
handshake and is the place to send a subscription RPC via
`pn_ws_send_text`; `text_message` is called for every inbound text
frame.  A subclass also sets `klass->normal_icon` / `klass->normal_color`
to override the default plug glyph and teal body.

Profile binding (ABI v6).  When the connection target lives in a host-
provisioned credentials profile (the typical case for an authenticated
service), the subclass declares its own profile-ref property and asks
the base class to drive everything off it:

      props[PROP_PROFILE] = g_param_spec_string (
              "endpoint-profile", "Endpoint profile",
              "Id of the my-ws-endpoint credential profile.",
              "", G_PARAM_READWRITE | G_PARAM_STATIC_STRINGS);
      pn_param_spec_set_profile_ref (props[PROP_PROFILE], "my-ws-endpoint");

      /* in _init or constructed */
      pn_ws_bind_profile (PN_WEBSOCKET (self), "endpoint-profile");

After `pn_ws_bind_profile`, the base class watches the bound property
and #PnVault::changed, resolves the profile through
`pn_node_get_profile`, and invokes #PnWebsocketClass::profile_resolved
on every change.  The default vfunc assigns the profile's "url" field
to `#PnWebsocket:url`, which kicks the existing reconnect loop -- so a
schema whose endpoint field is named "url" needs no resolver code at
all.  Subclasses that also need a secret (or whose schema spreads the
endpoint across multiple fields) override the vfunc:

      static void
      my_profile_resolved (PnWebsocket *base, PnProfile *p)
      {
          MyNode *self = MY_NODE (base);
          gchar  *url  = p ? pn_profile_get_string (p, "url")
                           : g_strdup ("");

          g_clear_pointer (&self->api_key, g_free);
          if (p) self->api_key = pn_profile_get_secret (p, "api-key");

          g_object_set (base, "url", url ? url : "", NULL);
          g_free (url);
      }

The same profile-edit live-update behaviour described in section 18
applies for free: editing the profile in the credentials manager
re-emits ::changed, the binding observes it, and the live connection
restarts against the new URL.  No code in the subclass.

`#PnWebsocket` lives in the GTK-free core, so a wss source node ships
as a single core-only `.so` with no companion -- the credentials picker
in the dialog comes from the profile-ref param-spec tag, which the
editor renders without the plugin shipping any dialog code.

The bump exists because the `profile_resolved` vfunc was appended to
`#PnWebsocketClass`; a stale plugin built against the smaller v5 class
must be rebuilt against v6.

Reporting connection failures (ABI v8).  Per section 12, plugins must
not rely on stdout/stderr to tell the user something went wrong --
those streams are not visible when pipnode is launched from a desktop
launcher.  The websocket base class therefore exposes a failure seam
that mirrors `connected` on the error side:

      static void
      my_connection_failed (PnWebsocket *self, const gchar *reason)
      {
          PnMessage *msg = pn_message_new (PN_NODE (self), NULL);
          gchar     *out = g_strdup_printf (
                  "Connection to %s failed: %s",
                  pn_ws_dup_url (self), reason);

          pn_message_set_boolean (msg, "success", FALSE);
          pn_message_set_string  (msg, "output",  out);
          pn_node_emit_message   (PN_NODE (self), msg);

          g_free (out);
          /* The base class continues its automatic backoff + reconnect,
           * so no retry scheduling here. */
      }

`connection_failed` is invoked on the main thread for every failure the
base class can detect: a malformed URL (synthetic reason "invalid URL"),
a handshake error (libsoup's #GError message -- DNS, refused, TLS, 4xx
on the upgrade), and a runtime protocol/transport error on a live
connection (also from libsoup's #GError).  The reason is always a
non-NULL, human-readable line suitable for the `output` member of a
failure #PnMessage.

The base class does NOT log the failure itself: a network source that
auto-reconnects forever would hit this path on every DNS hiccup or peer
restart, and a `g_warning` per retry would spam the journal.  The
emitted `PnMessage` from your override is the sole user-facing channel.
The base class still drives its own automatic backoff + reconnect, so
an override does NOT need to schedule retries.

The v8 bump exists because the `connection_failed` vfunc was appended
to `#PnWebsocketClass`; a stale plugin built against the smaller v7
class must be rebuilt against v8.


20. MQTT SINK / SOURCE BASE CLASSES
-----------------------------------

A device-specific MQTT node should derive from the bundled MQTT base
classes rather than opening its own libmosquitto client: `#PnMqtt`
(`<pipnode/pn-mqtt.h>`, the Source) and `#PnMqttSink`
(`<pipnode/pn-mqtt-sink.h>`, the Sink).  Both are
`G_DECLARE_DERIVABLE_TYPE`s in the GTK-free core.  The base owns the
broker connection, the `mqtt-broker` profile binding (section 18), the
automatic reconnect loop, the offline-publish backlog (Sink), and the
red "needs configuration / disconnected" body state; the subclass only
sees the messages and the errors.

Source (`#PnMqtt`) overrides two vfuncs:

      gboolean (*accept_topic)    (PnMqtt *self, const gchar *topic);
      gboolean (*process_message) (PnMqtt *self, PnMessage *message);

`accept_topic` runs on libmosquitto's network thread as soon as a
PUBLISH lands, before it is decoded -- return %FALSE to drop it cheaply
(the right hook for "ignore everything outside topic prefix X").
`process_message` runs on the main thread on the fully-built message --
mutate it in place to reshape, or return %FALSE to drop without
emitting.

Sink (`#PnMqttSink`) overrides two vfuncs:

      gboolean (*process_message) (PnMqttSink *self, PnMessage *message);
      void     (*report_error)    (PnMqttSink *self, const gchar *message);

`process_message` runs on the main thread on the inbound message BEFORE
the base resolves the publish topic / payload -- override it to stamp
the topic / payload a particular relay or bridge expects instead of
relying on an upstream `#PnRewrite`; return %FALSE to drop.

Sensing errors.  Per section 12 a plugin must not rely on stdout/stderr
to tell the user something went wrong.  The Sink funnels every error it
detects -- a rejected publish, a refused or failed connect, an invalid
broker URL, a TLS / auth setup failure -- through the `report_error`
vfunc, always on the main thread.  The default implementation records
the message in the per-node Log ring at `#PN_LOG_LEVEL_ERROR`
(`pn_node_log_error`), which surfaces in the per-node Log dialog.  A
subclass overrides it to react further (a device-specific note, its own
state, a failure `#PnMessage` on a node that carries an output port).

Chaining keeps a subclass future-proof if the defaults gain behaviour:
call up to `pn_mqtt_real_accept_topic` / `pn_mqtt_real_process_message`,
or `pn_mqtt_sink_real_process_message` /
`pn_mqtt_sink_real_report_error`, to extend rather than replace the
base.  Both `#PnMqttClass` and `#PnMqttSinkClass` carry reserved vfunc
slots so further extension points can land without breaking out-of-tree
subclasses.  Both bases live in the GTK-free core, so a device MQTT node
ships as a single core-only `.so`; the broker-profile picker in the
dialog comes from the profile-ref param-spec tag with no editor-side
code in the plugin.


21. REFERENCE PLUGINS IN THE TREE
---------------------------------

The source tree carries a handful of plugins you can read for
worked examples; they all build out of the box from a fresh
`./configure && make`.  Pick the one whose shape matches yours.

  - `tests/plugins/echo/`
        A minimal `noinst` plugin used by the plugin tests to
        verify the loader path end-to-end.  It is the reference
        TWO-TIER plugin (section 17): the GTK-free logic half
        `pn-echo.c` defines a single `PnEcho` pass-through node
        and exports `pn_plugin_init` (linked core-only, loaded by
        `pipnode-run`); the companion `pn-echo-gui.c` exports
        `pn_plugin_gui_init` and installs the node's settings
        dialog (combo editor + extra "Stats" page) for the editor.
        Read this first if you are writing your first plugin.

  - `plugins/network/`, `plugins/tasmota/`,
    `plugins/host-monitoring/`
        Bundled CORE-ONLY plugins: every node is pure logic, so the
        whole plugin is a single GTK-free `.so` that links
        `libpipnode-core` and installs on a headless server.  Read
        these for a plugin that registers several node types, links
        into autotools, and ships as part of the install.  (`image/`
        is the same shape and additionally pulls the allowed
        gdk-pixbuf core dep.)  `host-monitoring/` is the plain
        many-nodes-one-`.so` case: its eight "Host monitoring" probes
        (System Load, Net Connections, Disk I/O, Network I/O, CPU, CPU
        Temperature, Ambient Temperature, Memory) each sample /proc,
        /sys, or a remote sensor and emit a numeric reading.

  - `plugins/shell/`, `plugins/sound-effects/`, `plugins/ollama/`
        Bundled TWO-TIER plugins (section 17): a GTK-free logic
        `.so` plus a `-gui.so` companion for the one node whose
        dialog the schema cannot describe (a live `tmux` session
        combo; an async libsoup clip-download manager; an Ollama
        model picker populated from `/api/tags` with a Refresh
        button).  Read these for the real-world two-`.so`
        Makefile.am, the `g_type_from_name` type lookup, and -- in
        sound-effects -- a helper file shared by both halves.
        `ollama/` is the simplest of the three: the GTK-only
        `/api/tags` query is used by nothing but the dialog, so it
        lives wholly in the companion rather than being shared.

  - Built-in nodes using the schema (section 16): `lib/pn-led.c`
        (one greyed-out row) and `lib/pn-dial.c` /
        `lib/pn-analog-meter.c` (tabbed schemas).  Not plugins, but
        the clearest worked examples of `PnSettingsSchema`.


22. QUICK CHECKLIST
-------------------

Before publishing a plugin, walk through this list:

  [ ] One C file (or several, your call) defining your
      `PnNode` subclasses with `G_DEFINE_TYPE`.
  [ ] `_class_init` sets `class_name`, `icon`, `color`,
      `category`, `has_input`, `has_output`, and `receive`.
  [ ] One translation unit exporting `pn_plugin_init` with
      `G_MODULE_EXPORT`.
  [ ] `PnPluginInfo` has `abi_version = PN_PLUGIN_ABI_VERSION`
      and a non-empty `name` and `version`.
  [ ] Each node type your plugin provides is registered with
      `pn_node_factory_register` from `pn_plugin_init`.
  [ ] Built as a `.so` (no `lib` prefix, no version suffix)
      that resolves with `ldd` and exposes `pn_plugin_init`
      under `nm -D`.
  [ ] Installed into one of the directories from section 7,
      typically `~/.local/share/pipnode/plugins/` for an
      end-user install.
  [ ] Verified with `G_MESSAGES_DEBUG=all pipnode` that the
      loader logs `loaded plugin "<name>" <version>`.
  [ ] Verified that the new node appears in the palette and
      can be saved into a worksheet, closed, and reloaded.
  [ ] One `help/<TypeName>.html` file per registered node type,
      installed via your Makefile.am into `$(pkgdatadir)/help/`
      (see section 14).  Optional but strongly recommended:
      without it the palette's right-click Help falls back to
      the host index.
  [ ] If a node needs a host, secret, or permission: declare a
      profile type (section 18) and register it with
      `pn_node_factory_register_profile_type`, tag the referencing
      property with `pn_param_spec_set_profile_ref`, resolve through
      `pn_node_get_profile`, and `abi_version` is >= 5.  Do NOT store
      a secret in a plain serialised property (tag any legacy inline
      one with `pn_param_spec_set_secret` so it never reaches the file).

If every box is ticked, you are done.

To make a plugin SERVER-INSTALLABLE (it runs under `pipnode-run` on a
host with no GTK), walk this extra list:

  [ ] No node sets a GTK vfunc (`build_property_editor`,
      `build_class_tab`, `build_class_tabs`, `build_extra_pages`,
      `paint_*`) and the plugin includes no `<gtk/...>` /
      `<cairo.h>` / GtkSourceView headers.
  [ ] Makefile.am links `libpipnode-core` (not `-gui`) and uses
      `$(GLIB_CFLAGS)` rather than `$(GTK_CFLAGS)`; add
      `$(GDK_PIXBUF_*)` only if you handle image-message pixbufs.
  [ ] `objdump -p myplugin.so | grep NEEDED` shows
      `libpipnode-core` and no gtk/gdk/cairo/pango.
  [ ] Verified the `.so` loads under `pipnode-run` and a worksheet
      using your node runs headless.

  If your node DOES need a custom dialog, first try to express it as a
  declarative schema (section 16) -- that keeps everything in the one
  core `.so`.  Only when the schema cannot describe it do you split off
  a companion (section 17):

  [ ] Companion `myplugin-gui.so` links the GTK tier, exports
      `pn_plugin_gui_init`, and resolves its types with
      `g_type_from_name` (never the logic header's type macro).


22. PLUGIN LICENSING AND THE PLUGIN EXCEPTION

The pipnode core (the `src/` application and the `lib/` host library)
is licensed under the GNU GPL version 3 or later.  Because a plugin
links against `libpipnode` and uses pipnode's types and functions, a
plugin would normally be a derivative work of the core and would
therefore have to be GPL as well.

Pipnode grants an explicit exception so that this is NOT the case.
The file `LICENSE.PLUGIN-EXCEPTION` (an additional permission under
GPL v3 section 7) allows any plugin that talks to pipnode only through
the documented plugin interface to be released under ANY license,
including a proprietary one.  In short:

  - You may publish your plugin under any terms you like -- GPL,
    MIT, a commercial EULA, or no public license at all (a private
    binary).  This applies to everyone, not just the pipnode author.

  - The exception covers whatever your plugin extends: node types,
    and any future plugin kinds documented here (for example
    worksheet storage / serialization back-ends or export formats).

  - The condition is that the plugin interacts with pipnode SOLELY
    through the plugin interface described in this file -- the
    `pn_plugin_init` entry point and the public functions and types
    in the installed pipnode headers (`pn-plugin.h`, `pn-node.h`,
    `pn-node-factory.h`, `pn-message.h`, and the others).  Do not
    reach into private struct internals to stay clearly within the
    exception.

  - If instead you MODIFY the pipnode core itself (anything in
    `src/` or `lib/`), those modifications are still governed by the
    GPL.  Adding a new host-side extension point is a core change and
    is therefore GPL; the plugin that uses it can still be
    proprietary.

This is why the project can ship some plugins as paid, sponsor-only
binaries while keeping the core fully open, and why you are free to do
the same with your own plugins.  See the top-level `LICENSE`,
`COPYING`, and `LICENSE.PLUGIN-EXCEPTION` for the exact terms.
